<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hiroshima</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hiroshima is a city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>. On 6 August 1945 it became the first city to be destroyed by an atomic bomb, dropped by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>. The bombing, followed by that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasaki">Nagasaki</a>, preceded Japan's surrender and the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hiroshima.jpg" alt="Hiroshima" width="320" height="205" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: DXR · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Atomic_Bomb_Dome_and_Motoyaso_River,_Hiroshima,_Northwest_view_20190417_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The atomic bombing of <b>Hiroshima</b> on 6 August 1945 and Nagasaki on 9 August, combined with the Soviet declaration of war on 8 August and the rapid destruction of the Kwantung Army in Manchuria, finally provided Emperor Hirohito with the external shock necessary to override institutional resistance to…</blockquote><a href="/empire/empire-of-japan">Empire of Japan · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>…Front in 1918, and in the Second World War, which the United States entered after the Japanese attack on Pearl Harbor on 7 December 1941, its industry and forces fought in Europe and the Pacific, and the war ended after atomic bombs were dropped on <b>Hiroshima</b> and Nagasaki.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-states">United States · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1999, after a <b>Hiroshima</b> school principal killed himself over a dispute between his school board and his teachers about the flag and anthem, the government of Keizō Obuchi passed the Act on National Flag and Anthem, enacted on 13 August 1999 after a 403 to 86 vote…</blockquote><a href="/songs/kimigayo">Kimigayo</a></li><li><blockquote>The B-29 Enola Gay dropped a uranium bomb on <b>Hiroshima</b>, the first nuclear weapon used in war.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-6">6 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Its yield of about 50 megatons was some 3,000 times that of the <b>Hiroshima</b> bomb.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-30">30 October in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1619 the shogunate dismissed Masanori for repairing the flood-damaged castle without permission, and transferred Asano Nagaakira to <b>Hiroshima</b> with about 426,000 koku.</blockquote><a href="/state/hiroshima-domain">Hiroshima Domain</a></li></ul></section>
